---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { site } from "../lib/site";
---
<Base title="How this website works" description="Operations manual for brbcbaytown.org" noindex={true}>
  <div class="phead">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">Internal · unlisted</p>
      <h1>How this website works.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Everything a person who knows nothing about this site needs in order to run it,
        fix it, or hand it to someone else. Written 7 September 2026, the day it went live.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section class="sec"><div class="wrap doc">
    <div class="callout warn">
      <p><strong>This page is unlisted, not private.</strong> It is not linked from the menu,
      the footer, or the sitemap, and search engines are told not to index it. But anyone
      who has the address can read it. So it names every account and says where the keys
      are kept — <strong>it never contains a password, token, or key itself.</strong> Do not add one.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>On this page</h3>
    <ol class="toc">
      <li><a href="#version">The one-minute version</a></li>
      <li><a href="#map">Every system, and who owns it</a></li>
      <li><a href="#accounts">The two email accounts</a></li>
      <li><a href="#flow">How a change gets onto the site</a></li>
      <li><a href="#tina">Editing the site with Tina, step by step</a></li>
      <li><a href="#events">Adding an event (this is Planning Center, not Tina)</a></li>
      <li><a href="#sermons">Sermons are automatic</a></li>
      <li><a href="#broken">When something breaks</a></li>
      <li><a href="#calendar">The yearly calendar</a></li>
      <li><a href="#rules">Rules that keep it from rotting</a></li>
      <li><a href="#docs">The written documents</a></li>
      <li><a href="#open">Still open</a></li>
    </ol>
  </div></section>

  <section class="sec" id="version"><div class="wrap doc">
    <h2>1. The one-minute version</h2>
    <p><strong>brbcbaytown.org</strong> is a nine-page website. Its words live as plain text
    files in a code repository on GitHub. When somebody edits a word through the editor at
    <code>brbcbaytown.org/admin</code>, that saves a change to GitHub, GitHub tells Cloudflare,
    Cloudflare rebuilds the site in about two minutes, and the change is live. Nobody presses
    "publish". There is no database and no server to log into.</p>
    <p>Two things update themselves: <strong>sermons</strong> arrive from the church's YouTube
    channel every Monday, and <strong>events</strong> arrive from Planning Center every night —
    but only events someone has tagged <code>Website</code>. Giving runs through Subsplash. The
    two forms — "I'm interested" and "Join the serve team" — are Planning Center forms.</p>
    <p>It costs nothing to run. The only paid thing is the domain name, renewed every few years.</p>
  </div></section>

  <section class="sec" id="map"><div class="wrap doc">
    <h2>2. Every system, and who owns it</h2>
    <p>Ten pieces. If you can find all ten, you have the whole site.</p>
    <div class="tblwrap"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Piece</th><th>What it does</th><th>Where</th><th>Which account</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Domain name</strong></td><td>Owns <code>brbcbaytown.org</code>. Expires <strong>5 Feb 2028</strong>. Its nameservers point at Cloudflare.</td><td>Network Solutions</td><td>The church's registrar login</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>DNS + hosting</strong></td><td>Answers the domain and serves the site. Set up as a Worker named <code>website</code> with static assets. Rebuilds on every GitHub push.</td><td>dash.cloudflare.com</td><td>bakerroadbaptist@gmail.com</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>The code</strong></td><td>Every page, photo, and setting. Branch <code>main</code> is what is live. Public repository.</td><td>github.com/bakerroad/website</td><td>bakerroadbaptist@gmail.com</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>The editor</strong></td><td>TinaCloud. The thing behind <code>/admin</code>. Free plan — <strong>exactly two editor seats.</strong></td><td>app.tina.io · project "website"</td><td>bakerroadbaptist@gmail.com</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Sermons</strong></td><td>YouTube channel <code>@BRBCBaytown</code>. A weekly job copies new videos onto the Watch page.</td><td>youtube.com/@BRBCBaytown</td><td>The church's YouTube</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Events</strong></td><td>Planning Center Calendar. Only events tagged <code>Website</code> reach the site.</td><td>calendar.planningcenteronline.com</td><td>The church's Planning Center</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Forms</strong></td><td>Two Planning Center forms: <em>Connection Card</em> (1314833) and <em>Join the Team</em> (1314832). Submissions land in Planning Center People.</td><td>people.planningcenteronline.com → Forms</td><td>The church's Planning Center</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Analytics</strong></td><td>Google Analytics 4, property <code>G-37WGNT2TFM</code>, on every public page (not on this one). Counts visits; sets its own cookies — disclosed in the privacy policy.</td><td>analytics.google.com</td><td>The Google account that owns the property — confirm it is bakerroadbaptist@gmail.com</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Giving</strong></td><td>Subsplash. Embedded on the Give page. The website never touches payment details.</td><td>subsplash.com</td><td>The church's Subsplash</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Automation keys</strong></td><td>Two secrets the events robot needs (Planning Center ID + secret), stored as encrypted GitHub Actions secrets. Sermons need no key: they use YouTube's free RSS feed. A <code>YOUTUBE_API_KEY</code> is optional and only pulls the old back catalogue.</td><td>GitHub → repo → Settings → Secrets</td><td>bakerroadbaptist@gmail.com</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Church email</strong></td><td>Where every "Email us" button on the site goes. Also Sarah Rose's login for the editor.</td><td>Gmail</td><td>bakerroadbc@gmail.com</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
    <div class="callout">
      <p><strong>Where the actual passwords and keys are.</strong> Not here. They are on Matt Rose's
      Mac in a locked folder beside the project (<code>Baker Road Baptist/STORED ACCOUNTS/</code>),
      and the automation keys are in GitHub Secrets and Cloudflare's build variables, which cannot
      be read back once saved. The right long-term home is a shared password manager. If you are
      taking this over and cannot reach Matt, every one of these services has a normal
      "forgot password" flow on the account email above.</p>
    </div>
  </div></section>

  <section class="sec" id="accounts"><div class="wrap doc">
    <h2>3. The two email accounts</h2>
    <p>Two Gmail addresses, two jobs. Confusing them is the most common way to get locked out.</p>
    <div class="tblwrap"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Address</th><th>Job</th><th>Owns</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>bakerroadbaptist@gmail.com</code></td><td><strong>Build account.</strong> The infrastructure. Created for this project.</td><td>GitHub, Cloudflare, TinaCloud project, Google Cloud (YouTube API key)</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>bakerroadbc@gmail.com</code></td><td><strong>The church's account.</strong> Public-facing.</td><td>Receives every "Email us" on the site. Sarah Rose's editor login.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
    <p><strong>Turn on two-factor authentication on both</strong>, and write the recovery codes
    down on paper somewhere in the church office. The realistic disaster is not a hacker; it is
    one person losing a phone and the church losing access to its own website.</p>
    <p>Add a second person as an admin on GitHub and Cloudflare. A church should never be one
    login away from losing its site.</p>
  </div></section>

  <section class="sec" id="flow"><div class="wrap doc">
    <h2>4. How a change gets onto the site</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Someone edits a page at <code>brbcbaytown.org/admin</code> and clicks <strong>Save</strong>.</li>
      <li>Tina writes that change into the code repository on GitHub as a commit.</li>
      <li>GitHub tells Cloudflare a change landed.</li>
      <li>Cloudflare rebuilds the whole site from scratch. Takes about two minutes.</li>
      <li>The new version goes live. Your browser may still show the old one for a minute — hard-refresh
        (<code>Cmd+Shift+R</code> on a Mac) or open a private window.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>You can watch step 4 happen at Cloudflare → Workers &amp; Pages → website → Deployments.
    A green check means it worked. A red X means the build failed and the site is still showing
    the previous version — see <a href="#broken">when something breaks</a>.</p>
    <div class="callout"><p><strong>There is no "publish" button, on purpose.</strong> Save is publish.
    If you save something by mistake, saving it back is the undo. Every version ever saved is kept in
    GitHub's history, so nothing is ever truly lost.</p></div>
  </div></section>

  <section class="sec" id="tina"><div class="wrap doc">
    <h2>5. Editing the site with Tina, step by step</h2>
    <p>Written for whoever ends up doing this. You do not need to know anything about computers
    beyond filling in a form.</p>

    <h3>Getting in</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Go to <strong>brbcbaytown.org/admin</strong>.</li>
      <li>Click <strong>Log in with TinaCloud</strong>. Use the email address that was invited as an
        editor. If you have not been invited, whoever runs <code>bakerroadbaptist@gmail.com</code> adds
        you at app.tina.io → project → Collaborators. <strong>There are only two seats</strong> on the
        free plan; a third costs money. Share a seat rather than buy one.</li>
      <li>You land on "Welcome to Tina!" with a menu on the left. That menu is the entire website.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>The four things in the left menu</h3>
    <div class="tblwrap"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Menu item</th><th>What is in it</th><th>Edit it?</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>1. Church Info</strong></td><td>Address, phone, office hours, the Sunday and Wednesday schedules, links to Facebook, YouTube, giving, the app, and the two forms. <strong>Change a time here and it changes everywhere on the site at once.</strong> This is the one you will use most.</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>2. Pages</strong></td><td>One entry per page: home, visit, about, ministries, watch, give, contact, privacy-policy. Every box is plain text.</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>3. Sermons</strong></td><td>One entry per sermon, written automatically from YouTube every Monday.</td><td>Only to fix a title or series. Do not add or delete.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>4. Events</strong></td><td>Written automatically from Planning Center every night.</td><td>Only to tick "show on homepage" or add a picture. Do not add or delete — see <a href="#events">§6</a>.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>

    <h3>To change a service time</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Left menu → <strong>1. Church Info</strong> → click the single entry.</li>
      <li>Scroll to <strong>Sunday schedule</strong> or <strong>Wednesday schedule</strong>. Each row has
        <em>What it is</em>, <em>Small note underneath</em>, and <em>Time</em>.</li>
      <li>Change the text. Click <strong>Save</strong> (top right). Wait two minutes. Hard-refresh the site.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>To change the words on a page</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Left menu → <strong>2. Pages</strong> → click the page.</li>
      <li>Each page has a <em>Headline</em>, an <em>Opening paragraph</em>, and a list of <em>Sections</em>.
        Click a section to open it. Inside a section are a heading, a paragraph, and sometimes a list of
        <em>items</em> — the cards you see on the site. Each card has a title, a small label, a description,
        a "when it meets" line, and up to two buttons.</li>
      <li>Type in the box like an email. Click <strong>Save</strong>.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>The two buttons on every ministry card</h3>
    <p>They are not typed in by hand. Each button's link is written as <code>@connectionCardUrl</code>
    or <code>@serveFormUrl</code>, which means "look this up in Church Info". So if Planning Center ever
    gives you a new form address, change it <strong>once</strong> in Church Info and all fourteen buttons
    follow. Other lookups that work the same way: <code>@mailto</code> (the church email),
    <code>@tel</code> (the phone), <code>@givingUrl</code>, <code>@facebookUrl</code>, <code>@youtubeUrl</code>,
    <code>@appUrl</code>, <code>@mapUrl</code>.</p>

    <h3>To add a picture</h3>
    <p>Only a few places take a picture: a card on a page, or an event. Open it, click the
    <em>Picture</em> field, upload. Landscape photos work best. <strong>Do not put a photo of a
    child on the site without a parent's permission</strong>, and do not use a photo somebody else
    took without asking them. As of launch, only the Pumpkin Patch has a real photo; the other
    ministries are waiting for someone to take one on a Sunday.</p>

    <h3>To fix a sermon that came across wrong</h3>
    <p>Left menu → <strong>3. Sermons</strong> → find it → fix the title or the series → Save.
    Your fix survives every future Monday sync; the robot notices a human changed it and leaves it
    alone. If a whole series is being missed, add its name to <code>content/sermon-series.json</code>
    in GitHub (that file is a list of known series names and their misspellings).</p>

    <h3>If a save does not appear</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Wait the full two minutes. Then hard-refresh.</li>
      <li>Cloudflare → website → Deployments. Is the top build green? If red, see <a href="#broken">§8</a>.</li>
      <li>GitHub → the repo → is your change the newest commit? If not, the save did not reach GitHub — log out of Tina and back in.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>What not to do in Tina</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Do not add or delete Sermons or Events by hand. The robots own those lists and will fight you.</li>
      <li>Do not change anything under <strong>Church Info</strong> that ends in <code>Url</code> unless the
        service itself gave you a new address.</li>
      <li>Do not go looking for a page builder or a layout tool. There is not one. Every field is a plain
        text box, deliberately, so that a form-filler can run this site.</li>
    </ul>
  </div></section>

  <section class="sec" id="events"><div class="wrap doc">
    <h2>6. Adding an event — this is Planning Center, not Tina</h2>
    <p>Events on the website come <strong>from Planning Center</strong>. You never type an event
    into Tina.</p>
    <ol>
      <li>In Planning Center Calendar, create the event as normal (name, dates, description).</li>
      <li>Under Settings → Church Center, set <strong>Visibility: Published</strong> and add a picture if
        you want one on the site.</li>
      <li>At the top of the event, <strong>Add tag → Website</strong>. <strong>This tag is the on-switch.</strong>
        No tag, no website.</li>
      <li>It appears on the site the next morning (the sync runs nightly, about 5–6 am).</li>
    </ol>
    <div class="callout"><p><strong>Why the tag.</strong> When the site was built, Planning Center's
    "visible in Church Center" box was ticked on 123 of 136 events — including two weddings, a
    memorial service, and the Personnel Committee. So the website ignores that box and publishes only
    what is deliberately tagged. It also refuses, by name, the outside groups that rent the building
    and anything that reads as a wedding, funeral, or committee. Weekly things — Sunday School, Bible
    study — are held back as "the schedule", which is already on every page.</p></div>
    <p><strong>Every September</strong>, someone adds this year's Pumpkin Patch to Planning Center and
    tags it. That is the whole yearly event workload.</p>
  </div></section>

  <section class="sec" id="sermons"><div class="wrap doc">
    <h2>7. Sermons are automatic</h2>
    <p>Every Monday around midday, a robot on GitHub reads the church's YouTube channel — its free public
    RSS feed, no key or account needed — and writes any new video onto the Watch page. It reads the date out of the title in whatever format it was typed,
    de-shouts ALL-CAPS titles, and sorts videos into series using the list in
    <code>content/sermon-series.json</code>. Only <strong>public</strong> videos are used — unlisted and
    private ones are skipped.</p>
    <p>Nothing to do week to week. If a title comes across wrong, fix it in Tina (§5). If the whole
    thing stops, see §8.</p>
  </div></section>

  <section class="sec" id="broken"><div class="wrap doc">
    <h2>8. When something breaks</h2>
    <div class="tblwrap"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Most likely cause</th><th>Fix</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Site is down / error page</strong></td><td>Domain expired, or Cloudflare custom domain removed.</td><td>Check the domain at Network Solutions (renews Feb 2028). Then Cloudflare → website → Domains: both <code>brbcbaytown.org</code> and <code>www</code> should be listed.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Site is fine but shows old content after a save</strong></td><td>Build failed, or your browser is caching.</td><td>Hard-refresh. Then Cloudflare → Deployments: if the top build is red, click <em>View build</em> and read the last lines of the log.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Build is red: "Missing clientId, token"</strong></td><td>Cloudflare's build variables were wiped or changed.</td><td>Cloudflare → website → Settings → Builds → Variables must have <code>PUBLIC_TINA_CLIENT_ID</code>, <code>TINA_TOKEN</code> (secret), <code>NODE_VERSION</code> = 22. Retry build.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Build is red for any other reason</strong></td><td>Something in the code.</td><td>Emergency valve: change the build command from <code>npm run build</code> to <code>npm run build:site</code>. That deploys the site without the editor, so the website is up while <code>/admin</code> is fixed. Then get help.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>/admin shows "Unexpected error validating your schema" or "Branch 'main' not found"</strong></td><td>TinaCloud lost its index of the code. Usually because <code>tina/tina-lock.json</code> was deleted, or someone clicked "Change Repo" in TinaCloud.</td><td>Make sure <code>tina/tina-lock.json</code> exists in the repo. In app.tina.io → Configuration → Refresh Branches; <code>main</code> should show a green check. <strong>Never click "Change Repo"</strong> — it replaces the whole project and invalidates every token.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>/admin login refused</strong></td><td>Not invited, or the two seats are full.</td><td>app.tina.io → Collaborators.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>New sermons stopped appearing</strong></td><td>The weekly job failed, or the video is unlisted/private (RSS only lists public videos).</td><td>GitHub → repo → Actions tab → "Sync sermons from YouTube". Red? Read the log. No key is involved — it reads the public RSS feed. You can run the job by hand from that tab.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Events page empty, or an event missing</strong></td><td>Not tagged <code>Website</code>, or the Planning Center token was revoked.</td><td>Check the tag first — it is almost always the tag. Otherwise Actions → "Sync events from Planning Center" and read the log. A new Personal Access Token from Planning Center goes into Secrets as <code>PCO_APP_ID</code> and <code>PCO_SECRET</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>A form button goes to a dead page</strong></td><td>Planning Center form deleted or renamed.</td><td>Get the new form address from Planning Center → Forms → View on Church Center. Paste it into Tina → Church Info → the matching <em>form link</em> field. All buttons update.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Giving panel blank</strong></td><td>Subsplash changed the embed address.</td><td>Subsplash → Giving → embed → new address into Tina → Church Info → Online giving link.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
    <p>Rolling back a bad change: GitHub → the repo → Commits → find the last good one → the ⋯ menu on
    any commit lets you revert it. Cloudflare rebuilds automatically.</p>
  </div></section>

  <section class="sec" id="calendar"><div class="wrap doc">
    <h2>9. The yearly calendar</h2>
    <p>This is the entire recurring workload. Put these on a real calendar.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Every September</strong> — add this year's Pumpkin Patch to Planning Center and tag it <code>Website</code>.</li>
      <li><strong>Every January</strong> — read the whole site once, out loud. Check every time, name, and role is still true. Pastor's bio, staff, deacons, class names.</li>
      <li><strong>Before 5 February 2028</strong> — renew the domain at Network Solutions. Set the reminder now. If it lapses, the whole site vanishes.</li>
      <li><strong>Whenever staff change</strong> — update Tina editors (two seats), GitHub and Cloudflare admins, and rotate the Planning Center token if the person who created it leaves.</li>
    </ul>
  </div></section>

  <section class="sec" id="rules"><div class="wrap doc">
    <h2>10. Rules that keep it from rotting</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>If a page will be wrong in six months unless somebody remembers to update it, it does not go on the website.</strong> It goes on Facebook or in the app. This rule is why the site went from 26 pages to 9 and why it will still be accurate in two years.</li>
      <li><strong>One name owns the site.</strong> Not a committee. Today that is Sarah Rose. Write the successor's name on paper when it changes.</li>
      <li><strong>Two Tina seats.</strong> Share one rather than pay for a third.</li>
      <li><strong>Never click "Change Repo" in TinaCloud.</strong> It does not refresh; it replaces.</li>
      <li><strong>Leave <code>--skip-cloud-checks</code> in the build command.</strong> It is what keeps the website deploying when the editor is misconfigured. A broken editor is a nuisance; a broken website is not.</li>
      <li><strong>The <code>STORED ACCOUNTS</code> folder never goes in the repository.</strong> It lives one folder above it for exactly that reason. Do not move it.</li>
      <li><strong>No passwords on this page.</strong> Ever.</li>
    </ul>
  </div></section>

